Why GPLv3 and not something more permissive
-------------------------------------------

The published firmware images are built with ESPHome, and ESPHome's C++
runtime - which every build links - is GPLv3:

    "The C++/runtime codebase of the ESPHome project (file extensions .c,
     .cpp, .h, .hpp, .tcc, .ino) are published under the GPLv3 license. The
     python codebase and all other parts of this codebase are published under
     the MIT license."
    -- https://github.com/esphome/esphome/blob/dev/LICENSE

So cgm-display.ota.bin and cgm-display.factory.bin are derivative works of
GPLv3 code no matter what this repository says. Licensing the whole project
GPLv3 makes the source and the binaries agree, rather than shipping an MIT
notice over files that are not MIT.

A practical consequence worth stating plainly: this licence does not stop
anyone selling this, and it cannot - GPLv3 forbids adding restrictions of that
kind, and ESPHome's licence is not ours to change. What it does require is
that anyone who distributes it, for money or not, passes on the complete
corresponding source under the same terms.


Third-party components
----------------------

ESPHome - https://github.com/esphome/esphome
    C++/runtime under GPLv3, Python tooling under MIT. Linked into every
    firmware image. Corresponding source is available from the project above;
    the exact version used for a build is printed in its own logs at boot.

ESPHome web UI - https://github.com/esphome/esphome-webserver
    Fetched at build time by build-webui.sh and served by the device so its
    configuration page works without internet access. Not redistributed in
    this repository; web-ui.js is generated and gitignored.

Roboto - https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Roboto
    Apache License 2.0. Fetched at build time and embedded as glyph bitmaps.

Material Design Icons webfont
    https://github.com/Templarian/MaterialDesign-Webfont
    Apache License 2.0. Fetched at build time; the trend arrows are drawn from
    it. Embedded as glyph bitmaps.


